│ ┏━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┓ │
│ ┃           Patch for 1-element tuple rendering issue            ┃ │
│ ┗━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┛ │
│                                                                    │
│ The issue arises from a rendering logic that removes the trailing  │
│ comma from 1-element tuples. The core problem is that the          │
│ rendering logic incorrectly assumes that a 1-element tuple should  │
│ not have a trailing comma, even though Python's tuple syntax       │
│ explicitly includes it.                                            │
│                                                                    │
│ ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── │
│                            ✅ Reasoning                            │
│                                                                    │
│ The rendering logic in the render_tuple method is responsible for  │
│ formatting tuples. The current logic removes the trailing comma    │
│ when the tuple has only one element, which is incorrect. The fix   │
│ involves modifying this logic to preserve the trailing comma for   │
│ 1-element tuples.                                                  │
│                                                                    │
│ ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── │
│                              📄 Patch                              │
│                                                                    │
│                                                                    │
│  [file]sphinx/_render.py[/file]                                    │
│  [original]                                                        │
│          if len(tup) == 1:                                         │
│              return tup[0]                                         │
│          else:                                                     │
│              return tup                                            │
│  [/original]                                                       │
│  [patched]                                                         │
│          if len(tup) == 1:                                         │
│              return f"{tup[0],}"                                   │
│          else:                                                     │
│              return tup                                            │
│  [/patched]                                                        │
│                                                                    │
│                                                                    │
│ ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── │
│                            Explanation                             │
│                                                                    │
│  • The original code removes the trailing comma from 1-element     │
│    tuples, which is incorrect.                                     │
│  • The patched code adds the trailing comma back when the tuple    │
│    has only one element, preserving the correct Python syntax.     │
│  • This change ensures that a 1-element tuple like (1,) is         │
│    rendered as (1,) instead of (1).                                │
│                                                                    │
│ This fix maintains the correct Python syntax while ensuring the    │
│ rendering logic aligns with the expected output.                   │
